WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:01.710
MARTIN OMANDER: Hey, Wes.

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.710
WESLEY CHUN: Hi, Martin.

00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:04.322
What are we doing today?

00:00:04.322 --> 00:00:06.780
MARTIN OMANDER: Well, last
week, you showed me your weather

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:07.793
alerting app.

00:00:07.793 --> 00:00:09.210
You said you'd
like to optimize it

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:11.460
by pulling the
values from a cache

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:14.310
instead of hitting an
external API every time.

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:15.060
WESLEY CHUN: Yeah.

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:17.100
And I read up on the
new CPU allocation

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:18.280
controls in Cloud Run.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:19.759
So let's do that.

00:00:19.759 --> 00:00:23.252
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:29.498 --> 00:00:30.790
MARTIN OMANDER: All right, Wes.

00:00:30.790 --> 00:00:33.403
Could you walk us through
your weather alerting app?

00:00:33.403 --> 00:00:34.570
WESLEY CHUN: Yes, of course.

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:37.180
When I enter a US state
abbreviation in this text

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:39.970
field, the app hits
api.weather.gov to get

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:41.480
the weather alerts
for that state

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:43.415
and then displays
them to the user.

00:00:43.415 --> 00:00:44.290
MARTIN OMANDER: Nice.

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:45.700
WESLEY CHUN: As you can
see, it's a web app.

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:47.770
And I didn't want to
maintain any servers

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:48.890
or virtual machines.

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:50.530
So I deployed it to Cloud Run.

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:51.917
It's running pretty well.

00:00:51.917 --> 00:00:54.250
MARTIN OMANDER: So Wes, I'd
imagine weather alerts don't

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:56.450
change second by second.

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:58.492
So is there an opportunity
to cache these alerts?

00:00:58.492 --> 00:00:59.242
WESLEY CHUN: Yeah.

00:00:59.242 --> 00:01:00.700
That's a great idea, Martin.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.650
By caching those
weather alerts, the app

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:06.620
can respond faster to users and
our bandwidth bill is reduced.

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:09.160
We also use up less computing
resources for our friends

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:10.840
at weather.gov.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:13.827
MARTIN OMANDER: Sounds
like a win-win-win.

00:01:13.827 --> 00:01:15.160
WESLEY CHUN: Yeah, that's right.

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:18.400
So I want to cache the weather
alerts for all 50 states.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.800
Well, I could schedule a
job with Cloud Scheduler

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:22.810
that updates this cache
every five minutes.

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:26.510
But now with Cloud Run,
and it's always on CPU,

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:27.790
we don't have to do that.

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:30.040
Normally, Cloud Run
throttles the CPU

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:31.720
after your service
has responded.

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:33.490
But with this new
feature, we can

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:36.430
keep the CPU at full capacity
for background tasks,

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:37.900
like updating my cache.

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:40.833
And on top of that, we get
a discounted rate, too.

00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:43.000
MARTIN OMANDER: I can see
how an always-on CPU would

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.510
be useful for other things
beyond updating caches, too.

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:49.150
For example, OpenTelemetry
tries to send

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:53.230
tracing data to the server
when the CPU is less busy.

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:54.940
WESLEY CHUN: Yeah, that's right.

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:57.520
This is useful for
[? goroutines, ?]

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:01.330
node async calls, Java threads,
Kotlin coroutines, and Spring

00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:02.998
Boot background functionality.

00:02:02.998 --> 00:02:04.040
MARTIN OMANDER: I'm sold.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:05.472
Let's do it.

00:02:05.472 --> 00:02:06.430
WESLEY CHUN: All right.

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:08.350
Well, first, I'll
edit my Cloud Run

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:11.470
service in the Cloud Console.

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:16.577
Then I select "CPU is always
allocated" and then redeploy.

00:02:16.577 --> 00:02:17.410
MARTIN OMANDER: Wow.

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:19.480
That was easy, Wes.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:20.230
WESLEY CHUN: Yeah.

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:22.440
And now in my code, I'm
going to add a loop here

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:24.810
that updates the cache
every five minutes.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:27.120
MARTIN OMANDER: And you've
implemented updateCache

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:28.773
further down in the code?

00:02:28.773 --> 00:02:29.940
WESLEY CHUN: Yep, down here.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:30.630
Take a look.

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:33.760
So I added all of this after
the standard get and post

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:34.720
processing.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:37.350
Any viewers who are interested
can see the implementations

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:38.925
in the linked repo.

00:02:38.925 --> 00:02:40.050
MARTIN OMANDER: Looks good.

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:42.110
Time to deploy?

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:42.890
WESLEY CHUN: Yep.

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:45.110
And here goes.

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:46.370
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:48.230
Looks like it's done.

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:51.120
I'll use the service to get
the weather list for California

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:53.470
now.

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:55.600
It looks like we
didn't break anything.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:58.350
What do the logs say?

00:02:58.350 --> 00:02:59.310
WESLEY CHUN: Let's see.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:01.050
Well, here, I see
that the cache is

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:04.080
being updated for all 50
states outside of any requests.

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:07.440
And here, I see that the service
returned a response to you

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:09.090
and that it was
read from the cache.

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:10.260
It's working.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:11.940
MARTIN OMANDER: Very nice.

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:14.012
How much will we pay for this?

00:03:14.012 --> 00:03:15.470
WESLEY CHUN: Well,
regarding costs,

00:03:15.470 --> 00:03:18.890
note that we are being billed
for more time, meaning rather

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:21.830
than just the time when
instances respond to requests,

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:25.250
we're now incurring costs for
an entire instance's lifetime,

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:26.930
meaning from when a
container is started

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:28.220
to when it's terminated.

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:30.290
On the other hand, we
get a big discount--

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:33.980
25% less for CPU,
20% less for memory--

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:35.960
and we won't be charged
per-request fees

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:38.540
for either requests
or background tasks.

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:39.770
MARTIN OMANDER: I like that.

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:43.730
How does CPU allocation
interact with Cloud autoscaling?

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:46.350
WESLEY CHUN: Well, Cloud Run's
autoscaling is still in effect

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:48.980
and may terminate container
instances if they aren't needed

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:50.600
to handle incoming traffic.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:52.520
Also, an instance
will get shut down

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:56.300
15 minutes after processing its
last request unless it's kept

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:58.190
active using main instances.

00:03:58.190 --> 00:03:59.900
Once your instance
gets shut down,

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:02.570
any background work
will also get killed.

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:06.290
In general, always-on CPU is
good for high and even loads

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:08.630
or if you want to
run background tasks.

00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:11.390
Those background tests could
be your own code or libraries

00:04:11.390 --> 00:04:13.642
like OpenTelemetry
or Spring Boot.

00:04:13.642 --> 00:04:14.850
MARTIN OMANDER: Good to know.

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:15.920
Thanks, Wes.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:18.649
And thank you for watching
all the way to the end.

00:04:18.649 --> 00:04:20.180
If you have any
questions for Wes

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:22.670
or me about CPU
allocation in Cloud Run

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:25.220
or feedback on this
episode or suggestions

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:27.620
for future serverless
topics, please let us

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:28.950
know in the comments.

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:30.410
Bye for now.

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:33.760
[MUSIC PLAYING]

